{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "Use Packer (or equivalent) to build the EC2 AMI in a reproducible, scripted way, baking in JDK 17, Kafka client, and standard performance-testing utilities (e.g., iperf, sysstat, sar, perf tools).",
    "Implement U2 and U3 as new Spring XD modules conforming to the existing Source/Sink SPI (MessageChannel-based, with module options metadata), so they are first-class XD modules discoverable via the module registry.",
    "Design the load-generator source to support configurable message rate, payload size, payload type (random/sequential), and a deterministic run duration so that benchmarks are reproducible.",
    "Design the load-receiving sink to compute rolling throughput (msg/sec, MB/sec), latency percentiles, and drop counts, exposing them via JMX and a periodic log line for offline analysis.",
    "For the Kafka offset-management fix (U5), move the Kafka source to explicit per-partition offset tracking backed by Kafka's consumer.commitSync() with an at-least-once guarantee, and add unit/integration tests that simulate rebalance and broker failover.",
    "For the Windows CI work (U4), use a Windows-based CI runner (e.g., a self-hosted Jenkins agent on an EC2 Windows instance or AppVeyor), and use AWS EC2 Windows AMIs to experiment with different base images until a green build is achieved.",
    "For the gh_pages site (U7), use Jekyll (the de-facto standard for Spring-style gh_pages) with the Asciidoctor toolchain to render the same source files as the reference docs.",
    "For the Lattice/Diego POC (U8), target Diego as the production container runtime (Lattice has been subsumed by Diego in Cloud Foundry) and prototype a small, pluggable ContainerRuntime SPI in XD with a Diego implementation and a 'local' default implementation.",
    "The performance characterization in U6 uses a simple linear stream (load-gen source -> passthrough processor -> load-recv sink) to isolate the runtime's overhead from business logic.",
    "All new modules and SPI changes are accompanied by module-level unit tests and a small set of integration tests executed as part of the standard XD test suite."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "A single developer (the estimator) performs all work; estimates are in productive workdays, not elapsed calendar days.",
    "Development workstation, source repository, AWS account, CI infrastructure access, and Spring XD source tree are already available and familiar; onboarding/learning Spring XD internals is not counted as a separate activity beyond what is embedded in each task.",
    "JDK 17+, Maven/Gradle, Kafka, and standard Linux/Windows tooling are licensed/available without procurement overhead.",
    "The 'developer' has prior experience with Spring XD's module SPI, Kafka clients, AWS, Jekyll/gh_pages, and Cloud Foundry Diego; otherwise estimates would need to grow.",
    "Estimate is for the actual software-development effort (design + code + test + debug + small documentation), not for full project-management overhead, requirements elicitation, or formal sign-off cycles.",
    "The Kafka offset-management fix is a focused bug fix; it is not a redesign of the entire Kafka source.",
    "The Lattice/Diego POC is a time-boxed spike whose output is a working prototype, design notes, and risk list, not a production-ready container runtime.",
    "The Windows CI task is scoped to 'isolate the remaining test failures' and 'experiment with new AMI images' for an otherwise-stable build pipeline; large test suites are not being newly written.",
    "Content for the gh_pages site (samples, tutorials, ref. architectures) is provided in draft form by other contributors; the developer's job is to organize, structure, and publish it."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "EC2 AMI build for Kafka performance tests (U1)",
      "activity_description": "Create a Packer-based (or equivalent) pipeline that produces an EC2 AMI pre-installed with JDK 17, Kafka client + broker, sysstat/iperf/perf tools, and XD runtime prerequisites. Validate by launching the AMI and running a smoke Kafka perf command."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "Load-generator _source_ module (U2)",
      "activity_description": "Implement a new Spring XD source module that produces messages at a configurable rate, payload size, and payload pattern. Add module metadata, unit tests, an integration test that runs the module inside an XD container, and a short README."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "Load-receiving _sink_ module (U3)",
      "activity_description": "Implement a new Spring XD sink module that receives messages, tracks counts, computes throughput (msg/sec and MB/sec) and latency stats, and exposes them via JMX and log output. Add unit tests, integration test, and a short README."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "Windows CI build setup and test-failure isolation (U4)",
      "activity_description": "Provision a Windows CI runner (self-hosted on EC2 Windows or via AppVeyor), schedule the XD build on it, then iteratively investigate, triage, and isolate the remaining Windows-specific test failures, including experimenting with different base Windows AMIs until a stable configuration is found."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "Kafka source offset-management fix (U5)",
      "activity_description": "Investigate the current offset-management behavior in the XD Kafka source, identify the inefficient/incorrect fetch path, implement a fix using explicit partition/offset tracking with proper commit semantics, and add regression tests covering rebalance and restart scenarios."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "Simple-stream performance characterization (U6)",
      "activity_description": "Design a minimal end-to-end stream (load-gen source -> identity processor -> load-recv sink), execute it against the new AMI for several configurations, collect throughput/latency numbers, and produce a short characterization report."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "gh_pages static site for XD collateral (U7)",
      "activity_description": "Set up a Jekyll-based gh_pages site (Asciidoctor plugin), define the information architecture (Samples, Tutorials, Links, Performance Benchmarks, Reference Architectures), ingest the provided content, and wire the site so it is auto-published from the main branch on push."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "Lattice/Diego pluggable-container-runtime POC (U8)",
      "activity_description": "Continue the POC by designing a small ContainerRuntime SPI in XD, implementing a Diego-backed implementation (and a default local-runtime implementation), running a sample XD stream on a Diego deployment, and producing a written scope/risks/design document."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "Cross-module integration and end-to-end testing",
      "activity_description": "Integrate the new source/sink modules with the new AMI and run end-to-end pipelines (including the U6 benchmark scenario) to confirm the modules, AMI, and the Kafka offset fix all work together; fix any cross-cutting issues discovered."
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "Release engineering and final documentation",
      "activity_description": "Bump module versions, write release notes for the increment, update the main XD README and gh_pages index to reference the new modules, the gh_pages site, and the POC outcomes, and tag the release."
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 50.0
  }
}
```

Note: the per-activity most-likely effort values (in workdays) used to compute the total of 50 are:
A1 = 3, A2 = 6, A3 = 5, A4 = 9, A5 = 4, A6 = 4, A7 = 6, A8 = 10, A9 = 2, A10 = 1 → 3+6+5+9+4+4+6+10+2+1 = 50 workdays.

